## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to reason about combined constraints and partial knowledge in a policy graph using three-valued logic; requires identifying a concrete satisfying assignment and developing a general propagation strategy for 3-valued logic with conjunction and implication operations.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene 3-valued logic semantics for implication (→), conjunction (∧), and disjunction (∨)
- Propagation of unknown (U) values through mixed logical operators
- Fixpoint computation with backtracking to avoid false (F) while preserving unknowns

## Code Example

```javascript
function imp(a,b){ // a->b in Kleene logic
  const not = v => v==='T'?'F': v==='F'?'T':'U';
  const or  = (x,y) => (x==='T' || y==='T') ? 'T' : (x==='U' || y==='U') ? 'U' : 'F';
  return or(not(a), b);
}

function propagate(rules, values){
  let changed;
  do{
    changed = false;
    for(const [name, expr] of rules){
      const newVal = expr(values);
      if(values[name] !== newVal){
        values[name] = newVal;
        changed = true;
      }
    }
  } while(changed);
  return values;
}
```

The algorithm iteratively propagates known values through constraints until a fixpoint is reached, ensuring no constraint evaluates to F while maintaining at least one U value.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of graph reachability and fixed-point semantics in logical implications, with practical applications in policy and permission systems.

## Key Concepts
- Upward-closure in partially ordered sets
- Fixed-point computation via forward-closure
- Linear-time graph traversal algorithms
- Cycle handling through visited state management

## Code Example
```javascript
function propagate(T, edges) {
  const trueSet = new Set(T);
  const queue = [...T];
  const adjacency = new Map();

  for (const [a, b] of edges) {
    if (!adjacency.has(a)) adjacency.set(a, []);
    adjacency.get(a).push(b);
  }

  while (queue.length > 0) {
    const x = queue.shift();
    for (const y of adjacency.get(x) || []) {
      if (!trueSet.has(y)) {
        trueSet.add(y);
        queue.push(y);
      }
    }
  }

  return trueSet;
}
```

## Example
Consider flags {A,B,C,D,E,F} with relations: A→B, B→C, C→A (cycle), A→D, D→E, E→F, B→F. If T={A}, propagation yields {A,B,C,D,E,F} as the least model containing T.

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests understanding of three-valued logic in a practical policy engine context, requiring the design of a backtracking propagator that navigates partial knowledge while avoiding false constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene three-valued logic (T > U > F)
- Implication as ¬A ∨ B under three-valued semantics
- Constraint propagation with Unknown values
- Backtracking search with pruning to avoid False outcomes

## Code Example

```javascript
// Kleene three-valued logic helpers
const T = 'T', F = 'F', U = 'U';
function not(x){ return x==='T'?'F': x==='F'?'T':'U'; }
function and(x,y){ return x===F||y===F?F : x===T&&y===T?T : U; }
function or(x,y){ return x===T||y===T?T : x===F&&y===F?F : U; }
function implies(x,y){ return or(not(x),y); }

// Backtracking propagation
function solve(){
  const vars = {F1:U,F2:U,F3:U,F4:U,F5:U};
  const constraints = [
    () => implies(vars.F1, vars.F2),
    () => implies(vars.F2, vars.F3),
    () => implies(or(vars.F3, vars.F4), vars.F5),
    () => implies(not(vars.F5), or(vars.F1, vars.F2))
  ];

  function backtrack(idx){
    if(idx === 5) return constraints.every(c => c() !== F);
    for(const val of [T,U,F]){
      vars['F'+(idx+1)] = val;
      if(constraints.every(c => c() !== F) && backtrack(idx+1)) return true;
    }
    return false;
  }

  return backtrack(0) ? vars : null;
}
```

## Sample Run

The algorithm explores assignments systematically, pruning branches where any constraint evaluates to False. It finds the solution F1=U, F2=T, F3=U, F4=U, F5=T, where all constraints evaluate to either True or Unknown, satisfying the requirement to avoid False outcomes.

